{"body_sha256": "a2310f8dfad47bb8484397b19746e0358291c16395fb0d46287b9c26b339e4a6", "final_url": "https://api.crossref.org/works/10.3115%2Fv1%2Fw14-4323", "ok": true, "retrieved_at": "2026-10-31T09:41:39.449245+00:00", "text": "{\"status\":\"ok\",\"message-type\":\"work\",\"message-version\":\"1.0.0\",\"message\":{\"indexed\":{\"date-parts\":[[2024,8,23]],\"date-time\":\"2024-08-23T18:32:11Z\",\"timestamp\":1724437931273},\"publisher-location\":\"Stroudsburg, PA, USA\",\"reference-count\":0,\"publisher\":\"Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"content-domain\":{\"domain\":[],\"crossmark-restriction\":false},\"short-container-title\":[],\"published-print\":{\"date-parts\":[[2014]]},\"DOI\":\"10.3115\\/v1\\/w14-4323\",\"type\":\"proceedings-article\",\"created\":{\"date-parts\":[[2015,6,17]],\"date-time\":\"2015-06-17T08:10:45Z\",\"timestamp\":1434528645000},\"source\":\"Crossref\",\"is-referenced-by-count\":4,\"title\":[\"Identifying Narrative Clause Types in Personal Stories\"],\"prefix\":\"10.18653\",\"author\":[{\"given\":\"Reid\",\"family\":\"Swanson\",\"sequence\":\"first\",\"affiliation\":[],\"role\":[{\"role\":\"author\",\"vocabulary\":\"crossref\"}]},{\"given\":\"Elahe\",\"family\":\"Rahimtoroghi\",\"sequence\":\"additional\",\"affiliation\":[],\"role\":[{\"role\":\"author\",\"vocabulary\":\"crossref\"}]},{\"given\":\"Thomas\",\"family\":\"Corcoran\",\"sequence\":\"additional\",\"affiliation\":[],\"role\":[{\"role\":\"author\",\"vocabulary\":\"crossref\"}]},{\"given\":\"Marilyn\",\"family\":\"Walker\",\"sequence\":\"additional\",\"affiliation\":[],\"role\":[{\"role\":\"author\",\"vocabulary\":\"crossref\"}]}],\"member\":\"1643\",\"event\":{\"name\":\"Proceedings of the 15th Annual Meeting of the Special Interest Group on Discourse and Dialogue (SIGDIAL)\",\"location\":\"Philadelphia, PA, U.S.A.\",\"start\":{\"date-parts\":[[2014,6]]},\"end\":{\"date-parts\":[[2014,6]]}},\"container-title\":[\"Proceedings of the 15th Annual Meeting of the Special Interest Group on Discourse and Dialogue (SIGDIAL)\"],\"original-title\":[],\"deposited\":{\"date-parts\":[[2015,10,26]],\"date-time\":\"2015-10-26T20:44:36Z\",\"timestamp\":1445892276000},\"score\":1,\"resource\":{\"primary\":{\"URL\":\"http:\\/\\/aclweb.org\\/anthology\\/W14-4323\"}},\"subtitle\":[],\"short-title\":[],\"issued\":{\"date-parts\":[[2014]]},\"references-count\":0,\"URL\":\"https:\\/\\/doi.org\\/10.3115\\/v1\\/w14-4323\",\"relation\":{},\"subject\":[],\"published\":{\"date-parts\":[[2014]]}}}", "truncated": false, "url": "https://api.crossref.org/works/10.3115%2Fv1%2Fw14-4323"}
{"body_sha256": "a989640f72a09c35f7bf139412f2ecb6de2b9e2a739cce37b666468a5c6bb249", "final_url": "https://api.crossref.org/works/10.3233%2F978-1-61499-672-9-1674", "ok": true, "retrieved_at": "2026-10-31T09:55:29.117043+00:00", "text": "{\"status\":\"ok\",\"message-type\":\"work\",\"message-version\":\"1.0.0\",\"message\":{\"indexed\":{\"date-parts\":[[2025,2,21]],\"date-time\":\"2025-02-21T05:25:26Z\",\"timestamp\":1740115526034,\"version\":\"3.37.3\"},\"reference-count\":0,\"publisher\":\"IOS Press\",\"content-domain\":{\"domain\":[],\"crossmark-restriction\":false},\"short-container-title\":[],\"published-print\":{\"date-parts\":[[2016]]},\"abstract\":\"<jats:p>Clustering is an underspecified task: there are no universal criteria for what makes a good clustering. This is especially true for relational data, where similarity can be based on the features of individuals, the relationships between them, or a mix of both. Existing methods for relational clustering have strong and often implicit biases in this respect. In this paper, we introduce a novel similarity measure for relational data. It is the first measure to incorporate a wide variety of types of similarity, including similarity of attributes, similarity of relational context, and proximity in a hypergraph. We experimentally evaluate how using this similarity affects the quality of clustering on very different types of datasets. The experiments demonstrate that (a) using this similarity in standard clustering methods consistently gives good results, whereas other measures work well only on datasets that match their bias; and (b) on most datasets, the novel similarity outperforms even the best among the existing ones.<\\/jats:p>\",\"DOI\":\"10.3233\\/978-1-61499-672-9-1674\",\"type\":\"book-chapter\",\"created\":{\"date-parts\":[[2025,2,20]],\"date-time\":\"2025-02-20T12:06:08Z\",\"timestamp\":1740053168000},\"source\":\"Crossref\",\"is-referenced-by-count\":0,\"title\":[\"An Efficient and Expressive Similarity Measure for Relational Clustering Using Neighbourhood Trees\"],\"prefix\":\"10.3233\",\"author\":[{\"family\":\"Duman&ccaron;i&cacute; Sebastijan\",\"sequence\":\"additional\",\"affiliation\":[],\"role\":[{\"role\":\"author\",\"vocabulary\":\"crossref\"}]},{\"family\":\"Blockeel Hendrik\",\"sequence\":\"additional\",\"affiliation\":[],\"role\":[{\"role\":\"author\",\"vocabulary\":\"crossref\"}]}],\"member\":\"7437\",\"container-title\":[\"Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence and Applications\",\"ECAI 2016\"],\"original-title\":[],\"deposited\":{\"date-parts\":[[2025,2,20]],\"date-time\":\"2025-02-20T12:37:07Z\",\"timestamp\":1740055027000},\"score\":1,\"resource\":{\"primary\":{\"URL\":\"https:\\/\\/www.medra.org\\/servlet\\/aliasResolver?alias=iospressISBN&isbn=978-1-61499-671-2&spage=1674&doi=10.3233\\/978-1-61499-672-9-1674\"}},\"subtitle\":[],\"short-title\":[],\"issued\":{\"date-parts\":[[2016]]},\"references-count\":0,\"URL\":\"https:\\/\\/doi.org\\/10.3233\\/978-1-61499-672-9-1674\",\"relation\":{},\"ISSN\":[\"0922-6389\"],\"issn-type\":[{\"value\":\"0922-6389\",\"type\":\"print\"}],\"subject\":[],\"published\":{\"date-parts\":[[2016]]}}}", "truncated": false, "url": "https://api.crossref.org/works/10.3233%2F978-1-61499-672-9-1674"}
{"body_sha256": "82bd8ee43d26357532d21b293a7d7fc9a207e24c1f61f4f1cd5da34ae9059686", "final_url": "https://api.crossref.org/works/10.4000%2Fbooks.aaccademia.2419", "ok": true, "retrieved_at": "2026-10-31T09:55:29.218223+00:00", "text": "{\"status\":\"ok\",\"message-type\":\"work\",\"message-version\":\"1.0.0\",\"message\":{\"indexed\":{\"date-parts\":[[2026,3,9]],\"date-time\":\"2026-03-09T21:23:18Z\",\"timestamp\":1773091398714,\"version\":\"3.50.1\"},\"publisher-location\":\"Torino\",\"reference-count\":2,\"publisher\":\"Accademia University Press\",\"isbn-type\":[{\"value\":\"9788899982942\",\"type\":\"electronic\"}],\"license\":[{\"start\":{\"date-parts\":[[2017,1,1]],\"date-time\":\"2017-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\"timestamp\":1483228800000},\"content-version\":\"unspecified\",\"delay-in-days\":0,\"URL\":\"https:\\/\\/creativecommons.org\\/licenses\\/by-nc-nd\\/4.0\\/\"}],\"content-domain\":{\"domain\":[],\"crossmark-restriction\":false},\"short-container-title\":[],\"published-print\":{\"date-parts\":[[2017]]},\"abstract\":\"<jats:p>Neural Machine Translation (NMT) has been shown to be more effective in translation tasks compared to the Phrase-Based Statistical Machine Translation (PBMT). However, NMT systems are limited in translating low-resource languages (LRL), due to the fact that neural methods require a large amount of parallel data to learn effective mappings between languages. In this work we show how so-called multilingual NMT can help to tackle the challenges associated with LRL translation. Multilingual NMT forces words and sub-words representation in a shared semantic space across multiple languages. This allows the model to utilize a positive parameter transfer between different languages, without changing the standard attentionbased encoder-decoder architecture and training modality. We run preliminary experiments with three languages (English, Italian, Romanian) covering six translation directions and show that for all available directions the multilingual approach, i.e. just one system covering all directions is comparable or even outperforms the single bilingual systems. Finally, our approach achieve competitive results also for language pairs not seen at training time using a pivoting (x-step) translation.<\\/jats:p>\",\"DOI\":\"10.4000\\/books.aaccademia.2419\",\"type\":\"book-chapter\",\"created\":{\"date-parts\":[[2018,4,20]],\"date-time\":\"2018-04-20T00:32:38Z\",\"timestamp\":1524184358000},\"page\":\"189-194\",\"source\":\"Crossref\",\"is-referenced-by-count\":3,\"title\":[\"Multilingual Neural Machine Translation for Low Resource Languages\"],\"prefix\":\"10.4000\",\"author\":[{\"given\":\"Surafel\\u00a0M.\",\"family\":\"Lakew\",\"sequence\":\"first\",\"affiliation\":[],\"role\":[{\"role\":\"author\",\"vocabulary\":\"crossref\"}]},{\"given\":\"Mattia\\u00a0A.\",\"family\":\"Di\\u00a0Gangi\",\"sequence\":\"additional\",\"affiliation\":[],\"role\":[{\"role\":\"author\",\"vocabulary\":\"crossref\"}]},{\"given\":\"Marcello\",\"family\":\"Federico\",\"sequence\":\"additional\",\"affiliation\":[],\"role\":[{\"role\":\"author\",\"vocabulary\":\"crossref\"}]}],\"member\":\"2399\",\"reference\":[{\"key\":\"ref0\",\"doi-asserted-by\":\"publisher\",\"DOI\":\"10.1162\\/tacl_a_00067\"},{\"key\":\"ref1\",\"doi-asserted-by\":\"publisher\",\"DOI\":\"10.1017\\/CBO9781139524537\"}],\"container-title\":[\"Proceedings of the Fourth Italian Conference on Computational Linguistics CLiC-it 2017\"],\"original-title\":[],\"language\":\"en\",\"deposited\":{\"date-parts\":[[2026,3,9]],\"date-time\":\"2026-03-09T11:35:30Z\",\"timestamp\":1773056130000},\"score\":1,\"resource\":{\"primary\":{\"URL\":\"https:\\/\\/books.openedition.org\\/aaccademia\\/2419\"}},\"subtitle\":[],\"short-title\":[],\"issued\":{\"date-parts\":[[2017]]},\"ISBN\":[\"9788899982942\"],\"references-count\":2,\"URL\":\"https:\\/\\/doi.org\\/10.4000\\/books.aaccademia.2419\",\"relation\":{},\"subject\":[],\"published\":{\"date-parts\":[[2017]]}}}", "truncated": false, "url": "https://api.crossref.org/works/10.4000%2Fbooks.aaccademia.2419"}
